@@ -0,0 +1,33 @@
+# Test process ownership
+
+Linux test commands run under `test/process_owner.py`, an ancestor child
+subreaper. It supplies ordinary orphan reaping even when a container's PID 1
+does not wait. The gate wraps each acceptance suite; the Python unittest runner
+also owns its direct invocations. For a native suite outside the gate, use:
+
+```sh
+python3 test/process_owner.py gleam test
+```
+
+The owner only waits for its own exited children while tests run. It does not
+change kernel detach/reconnect behavior, signal ladders, production liveness,
+assertions, permissions, sandboxing, namespaces, credentials, or host init.
+It cannot reap unrelated processes or zombies previously adopted by PID 1.
+
+The main command's exit code is retained; signal exits map to `128 + signal`.
+SIGINT/SIGTERM are forwarded to the owned command group. After command exit,
+children get a bounded two-second drain. Remaining live children are terminated
+and reaped, and a previously successful command is reported as a failure. A
+failed command remains failed. Interruption cleanup is also bounded. The caller
+must wait for this owner, as the gate and Python runner do.
+
+Non-Linux platforms exec the command directly and retain their normal init
+reaping. Linux subreaper setup failure is fatal; there is no fallback claiming
+these physical-absence assertions passed. A denied operation is an error.
+Success requires all of this owner's children to have been reaped. The owner
+cannot guarantee cleanup if it is itself killed with SIGKILL or if a child
+remains uninterruptible; those are failures, not passing cleanup verdicts.
+
+This fixes a test-environment dependency. Portable production-guaranteed
+physical absence without a functioning orphan reaper remains a separate
+process-lifecycle architecture requirement.
